Attribute Meaning When to use <code>method="GET"</code> Data in the URL: <code>?name=value</code> Search, filters <code>method="POST"</code> Data in the request body Creating/changing data <code>action="/url/"</code> Where to send it Always specify it, or Django will handle the current URL <code>enctype="multipart/form-data"</code> For files Required if there is an <code>&lt;input type="file"&gt;</code> <code>novalidate</code> Disables browser validation For testing or custom validation <p>🧠 Mental model: Non-semantic HTML is a city without street signs and street names. People (users) can still find their way by the buildings, but GPS (Google, Bing) and the navigator for blind people (the screen reader) are completely lost. Semantic HTML is the same city, but with proper street signs, district names and direction markers. Everyone finds their way instantly.</p> <p>📚 Why semantics matters for Google: Consider two variants: <pre><code>❌ &lt;div class="nav"&gt;               ✅ &lt;nav&gt;
     &lt;a href="/"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;               &lt;a href="/"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;
   &lt;/div&gt;                           &lt;/nav&gt;
</code></pre> Both look the same in the browser. But for Google: <code>&lt;div class="nav"&gt;</code> is just a block with the class "nav" (Google does not know what you meant). <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> is navigation. Google understands it as "here are links for navigating the site" and builds a better site map, determines link importance, and understands the structure.</p> <p>🌐 How a screen reader uses semantics: A screen reader (NVDA, VoiceOver) reads ARIA "roles". <code>&lt;header&gt;</code> has the role "banner". <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> — "navigation". <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> — "main". <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code> — "contentinfo". A blind user can press 'H' (headings), 'F' (forms), 'L' (lists), 'N' (navigation) — and the screen reader instantly jumps to the corresponding semantic block. With <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> this is impossible.</p> <p>❌ Common beginner mistake: Thinking that semantics is "a recommendation for the good kids". In reality: non-semantic markup = worse SEO ranking = less traffic = less money. In commercial projects, the difference in semantics can mean the difference in your position on Google.</p>

Method Example Reliability <code>By.ID</code> <code>By.ID, "login-btn"</code> The most reliable <code>By.NAME</code> <code>By.NAME, "username"</code> Excellent for forms <code>By.CSS_SELECTOR</code> <code>By.CSS_SELECTOR, ".note-card &gt; h2"</code> Powerful <code>By.XPATH</code> <code>By.XPATH, "//button[text()='Delete']"</code> Useful, but brittle <p>Add <code>id</code> or <code>data-test-id</code> attributes to the HTML specifically for tests:</p> <pre><code>&lt;button id="submit-note" type="submit"&gt;Save&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;a id="create-note-link" href="{% url 'note_create' %}"&gt;Create note&lt;/a&gt;
</code></pre>

Situation Mock? Why Email Yes We don't want real emails Payment gateway Yes We don't want real payments Weather API Yes We don't depend on the network <code>Note.objects.create()</code> No Better checked through the test database Form validation Usually not This is your real logic A small pure function No It's cheaper to call it for real <p>The rule:</p> <p>Mock the external boundaries of the system, not the whole system.</p> <p>A bad test:</p> <pre><code>@patch("notes.views.NoteForm")
@patch("notes.views.Note")
@patch("notes.views.send_email")
def test_note_create(mock_email, mock_note, mock_form):
    ...
</code></pre> <p>Almost everything is mocked here. Such a test can pass even if the real form, model, and view don't work together.</p> <p>Better:</p> <ul> <li>check the form and model for real through the test database;</li> <li>mock the email.</li> </ul>

Method What it checks Example <code>assertEqual(a, b)</code> <code>a == b</code> <code>self.assertEqual(result, 5)</code> <code>assertNotEqual(a, b)</code> <code>a != b</code> <code>self.assertNotEqual(status, "error")</code> <code>assertTrue(value)</code> The value is truthy <code>self.assertTrue(user.is_active)</code> <code>assertFalse(value)</code> The value is falsy <code>self.assertFalse(form.is_valid())</code> <code>assertIn(a, b)</code> <code>a</code> is inside <code>b</code> <code>self.assertIn("title", form.errors)</code> <code>assertIsNone(value)</code> The value is <code>None</code> <code>self.assertIsNone(result)</code> <code>assertRaises(Error)</code> The code must raise an error <code>with self.assertRaises(ValueError): ...</code> <p>An example with <code>assertIn</code>:</p> <pre><code>def test_error_message_contains_field_name(self):
    errors = {"title": ["This field is required."]}

    self.assertIn("title", errors)
</code></pre> <p>An example with <code>assertRaises</code>:</p> <pre><code>def parse_age(value):
    age = int(value)

    if age &lt; 0:
        raise ValueError("age cannot be negative")

    return age


class TestParseAge(unittest.TestCase):
    def test_negative_age_raises_error(self):
        with self.assertRaises(ValueError):
            parse_age("-5")
</code></pre>
